<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.T4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of DPO training with length-based preferences<span id="S5.T4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on accuracy (%) and latency (# tokens). The base SFT model is trained with <math id="S5.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>.
With DPO training, we further reduced latency by 70% without compromising the accuracy.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Thinking while Listening ‣ Can Speech LLMs Think while Listening?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we proposed two methods to teach the model to think while listening: first, based on Question Completeness (QC), <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>, and second, using DPO on reasoning traces generated with rejection sampling.
For the QC method, we can control the onset of CoT during training based on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a simple baseline, we trained the model by left-shifting the CoT by a fixed number of words of the user question. We refer to this as WS-<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, to denote shift by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> words.
Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2 Effect of early reasoning ‣ 5 Results ‣ Can Speech LLMs Think while Listening?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the accuracy v/s latency curves for our proposed methods as well as the baseline.
The latency metric is reported in terms of the number of tokens between the end of the user question and start of system response.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:32%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="S5.T5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Start CoT Gap</span> (# tokens) on the validation set. The gap is calculated by subtracting the position of <span id="S5.T5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;start_cot&gt;</span> between model generation and ground truth. </figcaption>
<div id="S5.T5.10" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:113.8pt;height:70.1pt;vertical-align:-32.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-2.9pt,1.8pt) scale(1.0,1.0) ;">
